<ServiceLayout
  serviceName="Brand Associations + CSV"
  headline="How does the market perceive your brand and what is it worth?"
  subhead="AI interviews with both current customers and non-customers reveal how every brand in your category is perceived, what associations each brand owns, and what your brand commands in dollar terms. Powered by Brand Density, Consumer Surplus Value, and a full emotional landscape analysis."
  description="KwantumLabs Brand Associations + CSV applies proprietary marketing science frameworks to measure what your brand is actually worth, where you own white space in the competitive landscape, and how customers perceive you differently from the broader market."
  buyingTriggers={[
    "You need to justify brand investment to a CFO who wants a number, not a sentiment score",
    "Your brand tracker reports awareness and favorability but can't tell you what those metrics are worth in revenue",
    "Internal debates about positioning stall because no one can agree on what the brand actually owns in customers' minds",
    "A competitor is gaining ground and you want to understand whether they are taking your positioning or filling a gap you left open",
    "You want to know whether your customers see you differently from the broader market, and what's worth amplifying",
    "You're planning a repositioning or brand refresh and need a baseline before you start",
  ]}
  deliverables={[
    { name: 'Consumer Surplus Value (CSV) scorecard', description: 'The economic premium your brand commands per customer, expressed as a dollar figure. Compared against key competitors so you can track whether you are gaining or losing pricing power.' },
    { name: 'Brand Density scores', description: 'A measure of how richly people can describe your brand vs. competitors. Brands people describe in depth command more loyalty and pricing power than brands people can barely describe.' },
    { name: 'Brand map (correspondence analysis)', description: 'A visual showing which brands cluster together and which occupy distinct territory in the competitive landscape. White space and positioning gaps are visible directly on the map.' },
    { name: 'Brand dimension index table', description: 'Over/under-index scores for every brand on every association dimension, showing exactly what your brand owns and where competitors are pulling ahead.' },
    { name: 'Emotional landscape analysis', description: 'Valence, distress, anxiety, empowerment, and self-efficacy scores for every brand in the competitive set, with statistically significant differences flagged. Broken out by customers vs. non-customers.' },
    { name: 'Unaided awareness ranking', description: 'Every brand respondents name without prompting, ranked by the percentage who mentioned each one, showing your share of mind in the full competitive context.' },
    { name: 'Customer vs. non-customer comparison', description: 'Side-by-side profiles showing how your current customers describe the brand vs. how the broader market sees you. The gap is your most actionable advocacy priority.' },
  ]}
  approach={[
    "Brand Associations + CSV is designed to give you a complete, quantified picture of your brand's position in the competitive landscape. Most brand research focuses on awareness and favorability, which are activity metrics. This study produces outcome metrics: how richly people can describe your brand (Brand Density), what it's worth in dollar terms (Consumer Surplus Value), and where you sit relative to every competitor on every dimension that matters.",
    "The study interviews two groups separately: your current customers and non-customers in your target market. This separation is intentional. Current customers tell you whether your brand promise is holding up after purchase. Non-customers tell you how the broader market perceives you before they've experienced your product. The gap between those two views is itself one of the most valuable findings: associations that are strong with customers but invisible to the broader market represent your highest-priority advocacy opportunities.",
    "Consumer Surplus Value (CSV) is a proprietary KwantumLabs methodology that links brand perception directly to the economic premium customers will pay. It is not a survey question asking how much someone would pay. It is a structural model that derives the premium from the pattern of brand associations, calibrated against actual category pricing. Brand Density complements CSV by measuring the richness of brand associations: brands that people describe in more detail and with more specific language command more loyalty and more pricing power than brands people can barely recall. Both metrics are trackable over time, which is why this study often leads to the Brand + CSV Tracking retainer.",
  ]}
  timeline="Most Brand Associations + CSV engagements are completed in 3-4 weeks from kickoff to final presentation."
  investment="Typical investment: $40,000-$75,000, depending on the number of brands benchmarked, audiences included, and depth of analysis."
  scopeNote="A study with one audience segment and 3-4 competitor brands sits at the lower end. Studies with multiple segments, 6+ brands, or international markets sit at the upper end."
  faqs={[
    { question: 'How is Consumer Surplus Value different from traditional brand equity metrics?', answer: 'Traditional brand equity metrics measure awareness, favorability, and consideration. These are useful activity indicators but they don\'t tell you what the brand is worth in dollar terms. Consumer Surplus Value derives the economic premium your brand commands over a generic alternative from the pattern of brand associations, calibrated against actual category pricing. It is a number you can present to a CFO and track over time.' },
    { question: 'What is Brand Density?', answer: 'Brand Density measures how richly people can describe your brand. It captures not just whether people know your brand but how much they can say about it, how specific their associations are, and how varied. Brands people describe in depth command more loyalty and pricing power than brands people remember but cannot describe beyond a basic attribute or two. KwantumLabs developed Brand Density over 25+ years of brand research.' },
    { question: 'Why do you interview both customers and non-customers?', answer: 'The two groups answer different questions. Current customers tell you whether your brand promise is holding up after purchase. Non-customers tell you how the broader market perceives you before they\'ve experienced your product. The gap between those two views tells you exactly what advocacy work needs to happen: which associations your customers own that the broader market hasn\'t heard yet.' },
    { question: 'How many competitor brands can you include?', answer: 'The study is designed to accommodate your full relevant competitive set. A standard study benchmarks 3-5 brands including yours. Studies with larger competitive sets can include more brands with a corresponding adjustment in scope and investment.' },
    { question: 'Does this study lead naturally to the Brand + CSV Tracking retainer?', answer: 'Yes. This study establishes the baseline. The Brand + CSV Tracking retainer repeats the core measurement wave over wave so you can see which associations are gaining or losing strength, whether competitive gaps are widening or closing, and whether brand investments are producing measurable outcomes. The study and the retainer are designed as a system.' },
    { question: 'What does the deliverable look like?', answer: 'You receive a strategic report with the CSV scorecard, Brand Density scores, the brand map, the dimension index table, the full emotional landscape analysis, and the customer vs. non-customer comparison. We present findings live and provide the underlying data for your team to explore further.' },
  ]}
